== Company financial difficulties ==
Hard times hit the company in 1995 and the company was bankrupt as a result of several factors:

* chairman Artioli's purchase of [[Lotus Cars]] for ~£30m ($50m)
* the company's expenditure to develop the [[Bugatti EB112|EB112]] four door saloon
* the missed payments to several suppliers since 1993.<ref name="The Independent">{{cite web |language=en |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/life-style/motoring/lotus-and-the-bugatti-connection-1579862.html |title=Lotus and the Bugatti connection |work=The Independent |date=28 October 1995 |access-date=27 September 2024 }}</ref><ref>[[#DT23|Trucco 2023]], p. 238</ref>
Dauer Racing GmbH of [[Nuremberg]], Germany, bought the semi-finished EB 110 cars in the assembly plant plus the parts inventory through the bankruptcy trustee. The remaining chassis were later developed by [[B Engineering]] into their [[Edonis]] sports car which uses the monocoque chassis of the EB 110 combined with a modified version of its engine, enlarged to 3.8 litres of displacement.
{{clear}}

== Specifications ==
[[File:Bugatti EB110 V12 (7175968010).jpg|thumb|{{center|the quad-turbocharged V12 engine used in the EB 110 GT}}]]
The car has a [[Multi-valve#Five valves|60-valve]], [[turbocharged|quad-turbocharged]] [[V12 engine]] fed through 12 individual [[Throttle body|throttle bodies]], powering all four wheels through a six-speed [[manual transmission]]. The {{cvt|3500|cc|L cuin|1|abbr=on}} engine has a bore x stroke of {{convert|81x56.6|mm|in|2|abbr=on}}.
The EB 110 GT had a power output of {{convert|412|kW|PS hp|0|abbr=on}} at 8,000&nbsp;rpm and {{cvt|611|Nm|lbft|0|abbr=on}} of torque at 3,750 rpm. 
The performance oriented Super Sport version had the engine tuned to a maximum power output of {{cvt|450|kW|PS hp|0|abbr=on}} at 8,250&nbsp;rpm and {{cvt|650|Nm|lbft|0|abbr=on}} of torque at 4,200 rpm.<ref name="GToff">[http://yo.spc.free.fr/Bugatti%20register/EB110_GT/GT_spec.jpg Bugatti EB110 GT]</ref><ref name="SSoff">[http://yo.spc.free.fr/Bugatti%20register/EB110_GT/SS_spec.jpg Bugatti EB110 Sport Stradale]</ref><ref>{{cite book| last= Davis| first= Tony| title= Lemon! 60 Heroic failures of motoring| year= 2004| publisher= Random House Australia| location= Sydney| isbn= 1-86325-494-3| page= 150}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.carfolio.com/specifications/models/car/?car=41789|title=Bugatti EB110 SS specifications |website=carfolio.com |access-date=26 February 2018 }}</ref>

The car uses a [[double wishbone suspension]] mounted to a chassis made from [[Carbon-fiber reinforced polymer|carbon fibre]] skins bonded to aluminium honeycomb. Equipped with Gandini's trademark [[scissor doors]], it has a glass engine cover that provides a view of the V12 engine. The GT is equipped with a speed-sensitive electronic rear wing and active air flaps near the rear window that can be raised at the flick of a switch manually, while the Super Sport has a fixed rear wing.

==Performance==
Official performance numbers for the Bugatti EB 110 GT are 0–{{convert|60|mph|0|abbr=on}} in 3.4 seconds, 0–{{convert|100|km/h|0|abbr=on}} in 3.46 seconds and a top speed of {{convert|212.5|mph|0|abbr=on|order=flip}}.<ref name="GToff"/>

''[[Auto, Motor und Sport]]'' tested a Bugatti EB 110 GT with its engine generating a power output of {{convert|412|kW|PS hp|0|abbr=on}} and published the following results:<ref name="AMS593">[[Auto, Motor und Sport]] 5/1993</ref>

* 0–{{convert|80|km/h|mi/h|0|abbr=on}}: 2.6 seconds
* 0–{{convert|100|km/h|mi/h|0|abbr=on}}: 3.6 seconds
* 0–{{convert|140|km/h|mi/h|0|abbr=on}}: 6.5 seconds
* 0–{{convert|180|km/h|mi/h|0|abbr=on}}: 10.8 seconds
* 0–{{convert|200|km/h|mi/h|0|abbr=on}}: 14.0 seconds
* Standing kilometre: 21.3 seconds
* Top speed: {{convert|336|km/h|mph|abbr=on}}

French automotive magazine [[Sport auto (France)|''Sport auto'']] recorded a 0–{{convert|100|km/h|mi/h|0|abbr=on}} acceleration in 3.5 seconds, 0–1000 m time of 21.2 seconds and a top speed of {{convert|338|km/h|mph|abbr=on}}.<ref name="SPA418">mentioned in sport auto (French) 418 November 1996</ref>

[[Autocar (magazine)|''Autocar'']] tested the EB 110 GT on 16 March 1994:<ref name="AC94">{{cite web |language=en |url=http://www.autocar.co.uk/CarReviews/RoadTestsData/Bugatti-EB-110-GT/204150/ |title=Road Test: Bugatti EB |publisher=AutoCar |date=16 March 1994 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070705232309/http://www.autocar.co.uk/CarReviews/RoadTestsData/Bugatti-EB-110-GT/204150/ |archive-date=5 July 2007 |access-date=28 December 2017 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

* 0–{{convert|30|mi/h|km/h|0|abbr=on}}: 2.1 seconds
* 0–{{convert|60|mi/h|km/h|0|abbr=on|order=flip}}: 4.5 seconds
* 0–{{convert|100|mi/h|km/h|0|abbr=on|order=flip}}: 9.6 seconds
* 0–{{convert|150|mi/h|km/h|0|abbr=on|order=flip}}: 23.2 seconds
* 0–400 metre: 12.8 seconds at {{convert|115|mph|km/h|abbr=on|order=flip}}
* 0–1000 metre: 22.9 seconds at {{convert|150|mph|km/h|0|abbr=on|order=flip}}
* Braking {{convert|60-0|mi/h|km/h|0|abbr=on|order=flip}}: 2.8 seconds

Official performance numbers for the Bugatti EB 110 SS are 0–{{convert|60|mph|0|abbr=on}} in 3.14 seconds, 0–{{convert|100|km/h|0|abbr=on}} in 3.26 seconds and a top speed of {{convert|220.6|mph|0|abbr=on|order=flip}}.<ref name="SSoff"/>

''[[Road & Track]]'' tested a Bugatti EB 110 Super Sport and published the following results:<ref name="RT94">[[Road & Track]] July 1994</ref>

* 0–{{convert|30|mi/h|km/h|0|abbr=on}}: 2.2 seconds
* 0–{{convert|40|mi/h|km/h|0|abbr=on}}: 2.7 seconds
* 0–{{convert|50|mi/h|km/h|0|abbr=on}}: 3.4 seconds
* 0–{{convert|60|mi/h|km/h|0|abbr=on}}: 4.4 seconds
* 0–{{convert|70|mi/h|km/h|0|abbr=on}}: 5.3 seconds
* 0–{{convert|80|mi/h|km/h|0|abbr=on}}: 6.8 seconds
* 0–{{convert|90|mi/h|km/h|0|abbr=on}}: 7.8 seconds
* 0–{{convert|100|mi/h|km/h|0|abbr=on}}: 9.1 seconds
* 0–{{convert|110|mi/h|km/h|0|abbr=on}}: 10.9 seconds
* 0–{{convert|120|mi/h|km/h|0|abbr=on}}: 12.6 seconds
* Standing {{frac|1|4}} mile (402 m): 12.5 seconds at {{convert|119.5|mph|km/h|abbr=on}}
* Braking {{convert|60-0|mph|0|abbr=on}}: 112&nbsp;ft
* Braking {{convert|80-0|mph|0|abbr=on}}: 209&nbsp;ft

The testers suspected that the acceleration numbers could be lowered considerably by using the clutch more recklessly than they did.<ref name="RT94"/>

French magazine [[Sport auto (France)|''Sport auto'']] measured 0–{{convert|100|km/h|mi/h|0|abbr=on}} in 3.3 seconds, 0–400 m in 11.0 seconds, 0–1000 m in 19.8 seconds and a top speed of {{convert|351|km/h|mph|abbr=on}} for the EB 110 Super Sport.<ref name="SPA410">sport auto (French) 410 March 1996</ref>

==Records==
On 24 May 1992 Jean-Philippe Vittecoq drove an EB 110 GT to an average speed (across 6 runs) of 342.7 km/h at Nardo' Proving Ground in Puglia, Italy, beating the official speed of 325 set by the [[Lamborghini Diablo]].

On 30 May 1993 Vittecoq drove a Bugatti EB 110 SS to 355 km/h again at Nardo'. For a short time this was a speed record, until it was surpassed by the [[McLaren F1]] which reached 386.4 km/h in 1998 at Ehra-Lessien, Germany, in the hands of [[Andy Wallace (racing driver)]].

On 3 July 1994 at Nardo' Loris Bicocchi drove an EB 110 GT powered by [[methane]] fuel to a speed of 344.7 km/h.

On 2 March 1995 Monegasque [[Gildo Pallanca Pastor]] drove an EB 110 with dedicated wheels and studded tyres in Oulu, Finland, reaching an average speed of 296.34 km/h and peak speed of 315 km/h.

During testing at the [[Nürburgring]] Nordschleife, the EB 110 prototype could complete the lap in a time of 7 minutes 44 seconds according to test driver Loris Bicocchi.
<ref name="Cironi Bicocchi">{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cU37VATS44c |title=Loris Bicocchi: King Mida of Supercars|first=Davide|last=Cironi|publisher=Drive Experience}}{{cbignore}}</ref>
This time was faster than that of 7:46.36 set unofficially by the [[Jaguar XJ220]]. 

==EB 110 continuation cars==
===<span class="anchor" id="Dauer"></span>Dauer EB 110===
[[File:Black Bugatti EB110.jpg|thumb|Dauer EB 110.]]
[[Dauer Sportwagen]] in [[Nuremberg]], Germany, bought the remaining stock of EB 110 parts from the Bugatti factory after the company went bankrupt in 1995. A complete spare parts catalogue, with exploded diagrams and part numbers was made by the company. Three Super Sport models and a GT model were finished between 1999 and 2000 with the Bugatti logo and minor modifications.

The remaining incomplete chassis were used between 2001 and 2002 to manufacture five significantly improved cars called the '''Dauer EB 110 Super Sport Light Weight'''. Power output was increased to {{convert|481|kW|PS hp|0|abbr=on}}, turbo lag was decreased and weight was reduced by {{convert|507|lbs|kg|0|abbr=on|order=flip}} with the use of a body made from carbon fibre. Top speed of the car was estimated at  {{convert|230|mph|abbr=on|order=flip}}, acceleration from a standstill to 97&nbsp;km/h takes 3.3 seconds and the car covers the standing kilometre in 19 seconds. A power output increase to {{convert|526|kW|PS hp|abbr=on}} with sports exhaust and modified ECU was available as factory option.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.classicdriver.com/de/car/bugatti/eb-110/2000/291948|title=2000 Bugatti EB 110 Super Sport - Dauer|access-date=3 February 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.motorauthority.com/news/1112046_a-rare-bugatti-eb110-ss-dauer-is-up-for-grabs-for1-16m|title=A rare Bugatti EB110 SS Dauer is up for grabs for $1.16M|date=11 August 2017|first=Jeff|last=Glucker|publisher=Motor Authority|access-date=1 February 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://yo.spc.free.fr/EB110_Dauer.htm |title=The Bugatti EB 110 Registry - The Dauer EB 110 Supersport |publisher=Yo.spc.free.fr |access-date=28 November 2010}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |last=Kuah |first=Ian |date=May 2002 |title=A second coming |magazine=Sports Car International}}</ref>

The company Dauer Sportwagen went bankrupt in 2008. All original Bugatti parts, including the high performance parts of the EB 110 Super Sport and the equipment, were bought in 2011 by the company Toscana-Motors GmbH located in Kaiserslautern, Germany.

==Cars based on the EB 110==

===B Engineering Edonis===
{{Main|B Engineering Edonis}}
The [[B Engineering Edonis]] is based on the Bugatti EB 110 Super Sport but has been extensively re-engineered, retaining little more than the [[Graphite-reinforced plastic|carbon-fibre]] [[chassis]] from the original Bugatti sports car. It was conceived by [[Nicola Materazzi]] and styled by Marc Deschamps and introduced in 2000 with a complete exterior and interior redesign.<ref name="Deschamps">{{cite web|url=https://justdrivethere.com/2012/11/22/the-edonis-the-forgotten-sports-car/|title=The Edonis The Forgotten Supercar|date=22 November 2012|publisher=JustDriveThere|access-date=7 May 2020}}</ref> The 3.5-litre Bugatti engine has had its [[engine displacement|displacement]] increased from 3,500&nbsp;cc to 3,760&nbsp;cc. The original four small IHI [[turbocharger]]s have been replaced by two larger units from the same manufacturer. Engine power has been increased from {{convert|450|kW|0|abbr=on}} and {{convert|650|Nm|lb.ft|0|abbr=on}} of torque to {{convert|507|kW|PS hp|0|abbr=on}} at 8,000&nbsp;rpm and {{convert|735|Nm|lb.ft|abbr=on}} of torque. A version with 720&nbsp;bhp set a speed of 359.6&nbsp;km/h at the Nardo' proving ground in Italy.
<ref>{{cite book
 | last= Phillips
 | first= Adam
 | title= Supercars: Driving the Dream
 | year= 2013
 | publisher= Rosen Publishing Group
 | location= New York
 | isbn= 9781448892136
 | page= 46
}}</ref>

In addition, the four-wheel-drive triple-differential drivetrain from the donor car has been replaced with a much simpler and lighter [[Front engine, rear-wheel drive layout|rear-wheel-drive]] [[transaxle]], thus saving approximately {{convert|70|kg|lb|0|abbr=on}} from the total weight. The car weighing {{convert|1500|kg|lb|0|abbr=on}} has a [[power-to-weight ratio]] of 480&nbsp;hp/ton. In addition, the engine's specific power output is an unprecedented 181&nbsp;hp/litre. The brand claims a maximum speed of {{cvt|365|kph|mph|0|abbr=on}} while acceleration from 0 to {{convert|100|km/h|mi/h|0|abbr=on}} being achieved in 3.9 seconds. All changes were aimed at simplifying the vehicle.<ref name="Cironi2" />

B Engineering produced 2 prototypes of the Edonis (one in gold colour and one in 'Rosso Pompeiano' red) out of the parts retained by the company of the original EB 110 when Bugatti S.p.A. went bankrupt. The project had caught the interest of [[Hispano Suiza]] but the discussions broke down over the valuation of the project in view of a sale.<ref name="Cironi2">{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hVOEOEJAceI |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211222/hVOEOEJAceI |archive-date=2021-12-22 |url-status=live|title=Edonis e fallimento Bugatti|date=22 August 2017|first=Davide|last=Cironi|publisher=Drive Experience|access-date=7 May 2020}}{{cbignore}}</ref>

Apart from manufacturing the Edonis, B Engineering also provided spare parts and service for the original EB 110 until 2023.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.evo.co.uk/features/features/289450/az_supercars_b_engineering_edonis.html|title=A-Z Supercars: B. Engineering Edonis|date=13 May 2013|work=[[Evo (magazine)|Evo]]|access-date=2 February 2019}}</ref>

== Aftermarket modifications ==

[[File:Bugatti EB110 SS - Alain Cudini, Eric Helary & Jean-Christophe Boullion heads onto the pit straight at the 1994 Le (31822573952).jpg|thumb|Bugatti EB 110 LM on track at the [[1994 24 Hours of Le Mans]]]]
=== Brabus EB 110 ===
The Brabus EB 110 is a modified version of the EB 110 Super Sports by German automotive tuning company [[Brabus]]. The car has not received any exterior modifications by the company with most of the modifications done to the interior and engine. The car has a bi colour blue and black leather interior and a custom made quad pipe exhaust system. Engine modifications of the car along with its performance statistics remain unknown. This is the only EB 110 modified by the company. The car's existence became known only after it was put on sale in 2013.<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.roadandtrack.com/car-culture/classic-cars/news/a4398/classic-cars-bugatti-eb110-ss-by-brabus/|title=An already rare Bugatti supercar made even rarer|date=2 May 2013|first=Antti|last=Kautonen|magazine=[[Road & Track]]|access-date=1 February 2019}}</ref>

==Motorsport==
The EB 110 LM participated in the [[1994 24 Hours of Le Mans|24 Hours of Le Mans]] in 1994. The car qualified a very competitive 17th overall and 5th in the GT1 class but did not finish the race. The car is now on display at the [[Lohéac]] Automobile Museum. A second car commissioned by wealthy pharmaceutical entrepreneur Martino Finotto also participated in the 1996 [[24 Hours of Daytona]], but suffered a gearbox problem and did not finish.

